2. External backends (invoked as separate processes)
----------------------------------------------------

The AppImage and the macOS app bundle COLMAP and OpenMVS, built by
tools/backends/build.sh with all their dependencies linked statically, and
the Brush release binary. Their license texts are inside the app, in
backends/licenses/ (the copyright file of every library linked in), and the
app shows this file under Help -> Licenses.

COLMAP 4.2.1    BSD-3-Clause        https://github.com/colmap/colmap
OpenMVS v2.4.0  AGPL-3.0            https://github.com/cdcseacave/openMVS
                (depends on CGAL, parts of which are GPL-3.0+), with the
                upstream fix in tools/backends/patches applied
Brush 0.3.0     Apache-2.0          https://github.com/ArthurBrussee/brush
                The release binary, unmodified, with its LICENSE under
                backends/licenses/brush. Its Rust dependencies' notices are
                not in the release; collecting them (cargo-about) is a Phase
                6 release task.
GCC runtime     GPL-3.0 with the GCC Runtime Library Exception
                (libgomp, libgfortran, libquadmath; AppImage only, copied
                from the build machine for the backends)
libomp          Apache-2.0 with LLVM exceptions (macOS app only, Homebrew's)
FFmpeg          LGPL-2.1+ or GPL-2.0+ depending on build configuration
                                    https://ffmpeg.org/
                Used for video import (decoding, JPEG frames). Not bundled
                yet: the user's installed FFmpeg is run as a process.


Source for the bundled backends. COLMAP and OpenMVS in the apps are built
from the tags, the vcpkg release and the patches pinned in
tools/backends/build.sh, which records them in backends/BUILDINFO.json; all
of it is in this repository and the upstream projects. Development builds
(CI artifacts) point there. tools/backends/collect_sources.sh packs the
exact corresponding source (both projects, the patches, vcpkg and every
port's source archive; the Backend sources workflow builds it), and each
release publishes that archive next to the binaries. Anyone who got a binary
can also ask for it through the project's issue tracker.


